  - electricity markets
  - imbalance settlement
  - collateral
  - pre-registration
abstract: >-
  Reinforcement-learning (RL) traders for electricity markets train in simulators
  that decide who bears losses beyond capital. An energy-trading engine's protocol
  shortcuts ranked first a controller reporting +162% to +795% in all six runs while
  booking -1,822% to -3,655%: the engine floored cash at zero and forgave the rest,
  mostly unpaid costs. Such rules are options agents learn to exercise:
  floor-trained agents reported +38% to +148% while booking -15% to -57% in placebo
  markets, and in an unmodified public simulator with leverage and a linear reward,
  proximal policy optimization took maximal leverage at 89% of probes and went bust
  in 61% of episodes (3% and 2% with bankruptcy losses charged). The contribution in
  artificial intelligence is reading a simulator's accounting from its reward. Along
  the market input, on a clone, the reward is the payoff diagram of that option
  book: kinks locate floors and caps, tails give exposure, and booked profit follows
  without ledger, formula or account structure, given the own cost. We prove when
  this is exact, where it cannot be, and that error falls with the input's range,
  not the query count. A sign-reversed twin, its two-point case, brackets booked
  profit and yields rewards that kept 99% to 115% of the ledger-trained edge with
  losses floored per account or position, where a 5% balance ruin penalty failed. On
  real engine clones, the reading recovered booked return within 0.0001 points in
  ten runs; twin estimates erred by 9 to 1,410. The engineering application is
  validating RL simulators for electricity trading.
